---
/**
 * About.
 *
 * What the project is, and what the work in this repository actually consists of. No
 * affiliation, job title, employer, education, years of experience or claimed proficiency
 * with any commercial package is stated, because the repository establishes none of them.
 * That is a constraint on what may be written here, not a subject for the page itself:
 * the reader is told what the project is, and the list of things it does not claim stays
 * in its own section below.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import { NAV, NOT_RUN, VERIFICATION } from "../../scripts/site";
import { href } from "../../lib/urls";

const contents = [
  { id: "work", label: "What was done here" },
  { id: "method", label: "How the work is run" },
  { id: "notclaimed", label: "What is not claimed" },
  { id: "licence", label: "Licence and citation" },
];

const description =
  "A personal project on gas-reservoir analysis and interpretation: reproducible calculations, synthetic case studies, how the work is run, and an explicit list of the things this site does not claim.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  nav={NAV}
  contents={contents}
>
  <h1>About this project</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Gas Reservoir Performance Lab is a personal project focused on gas-reservoir analysis
    and interpretation. It brings together reproducible calculations, synthetic case
    studies, and visual explanations of modelling assumptions and uncertainty. The studies
    do not represent an operator's field assessment or reserves certification.
  </p>

  <section id="work" aria-labelledby="work-h">
    <h2 id="work-h">What was done here</h2>

    <p>The author, writing as <strong>azul</strong>, built the following in this repository.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A dependency-free gas material-balance library.</strong> Pure Python, standard
        library only at runtime, covering gas properties and deviation-factor correlations,
        pseudopressure, depletion generators for the volumetric and water-drive cases, a
        Fetkovich aquifer, material-balance estimators, regression including
        errors-in-variables treatments, diagnostics, numerics, unit handling and a provenance
        record type.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Four pre-registered studies</strong>, each with a protocol written before the
        run and acceptance criteria fixed before the first execution: a volumetric baseline, a
        PVT check against reference equations of state, a measurement error budget, and the
        flagship counterexample. Three ship committed results; the fourth cannot, and the
        {" "}<a href={href("/studies/a2/")}>reason is on its own page</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A verification runner with two profiles</strong>, so that "the code agrees with
        itself" and "the code agrees with something it did not produce" are different reported
        outcomes rather than one aggregate. It counts what it skipped and fails on a skip its
        own policy does not explain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A presentation export that reconciles before it writes.</strong> It re-runs the
        study's own functions and checks {VERIFICATION.reconciliationChecks} quantities against
        the audited run summary at {VERIFICATION.reconcileTolerance} before producing a single
        figure data file, and it records the digest of the summary, of the study source and of
        itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>This site.</strong> No framework, no CDN, no analytics, no cookies, no remote
        fonts and no client-side charting. Every figure is rendered to static SVG before the
        build and inlined; the build fails rather than shipping a page whose exhibit is
        missing or stale.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The corrections.</strong> A repository-wide claims audit re-read every sentence
        against the run output and found arithmetic errors, mislabelled quantities and claims
        that exceeded their evidence. Those are marked in place across the studies rather than
        quietly rewritten, and several of them run against the author's own earlier
        conclusions.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="method" aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <h2 id="method-h">How the work is run</h2>

    <p>
      The working agreement in the repository is short and it is enforced by the scripts
      rather than by good intentions. Every numerical method declares units, datum,
      assumptions, validity domain, source and failure behaviour. Calculations live in the
      library, not in hidden notebook cells. Synthetic data stays labelled synthetic. A
      benchmark is not a field history match, and a shared input format is not evidence of
      experience with a different simulator.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three rules do most of the work. Do not claim runs that were not executed. Do not report
      author-written tests as independent validation. Do not rewrite a trusted reference result
      or widen its tolerance in the same unreviewed change as the code that failed it. An
      unevaluated command is a proposed command, not a successful run.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="note" title="Roles are not reviewers">
      <p>
        The repository defines separate roles — implementer, verification reviewer,
        interpretation reviewer, maintainer — and the author works through them in sequence.
        <strong> Different role names do not create independent expertise or independent
        evidence</strong>, and the repository says so in its own working agreement. Every
        review pass here was the same person under a different heading. Where earlier drafts
        described such a pass as "independent" or "external", both words have been withdrawn.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="notclaimed" aria-labelledby="notclaimed-h">
    <h2 id="notclaimed-h">What is not claimed</h2>

    <p>
      Listed rather than omitted, so that a reader can see the absence is deliberate.
    </p>

    <ul class="disclaimers">
      <li><strong>No field validation.</strong> {NOT_RUN.fieldData}</li>
      <li><strong>No external validation.</strong> {NOT_RUN.externalValidation}</li>
      <li><strong>No peer review.</strong> {NOT_RUN.peerReview}</li>
      <li><strong>Hosted continuous integration is not external validation.</strong> {NOT_RUN.hostedCi}</li>
      <li><strong>No deployment.</strong> {NOT_RUN.deployment}</li>
      <li>
        <strong>No employer, client, institution or project is named anywhere</strong>, and no
        endorsement by any data provider, software vendor or employer is implied.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No experience with any commercial reservoir simulator is claimed.</strong> The
        library reads and writes its own formats; a shared input format would not be evidence
        of such experience and none is offered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No priority claim.</strong> The flagship study's effect is documented in the
        literature and the study positions itself against that work rather than presenting
        itself as new.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No third-party dataset, simulator deck, book, paper or binary is
        redistributed.</strong> Where a study would use one, the repository stores retrieval
        instructions and checksums, and the third party's licence governs the material.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="licence" aria-labelledby="licence-h">
    <h2 id="licence-h">Licence and citation</h2>

    <p>
      The repository's own code and text are MIT licensed, copyright 2026 azul. That licence
      covers original repository material only. Referenced books, papers, software, datasets
      and trademarks retain their own licences and notices, and the repository's licence is
      not applied to imported material.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where a result depends on reference fluid properties from the NIST Chemistry WebBook,
      cite the WebBook and the per-species reference equation recorded in the repository's
      reference manifest. Those values are not published here; see the
      {" "}<a href={href("/studies/a2/")}>A2 availability page</a>.
    </p>

  </section>

  <style>
    .disclaimers li + li {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
